  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nUpper level ridging persists on Friday night, with a shortwave \ntracking through the upper level flow. This is expected to push a \nsurface low across the region, which may kick up a few showers and \npotentially some thunderstorms overnight. Ensemble guidance \ncontinues to suggest that these showers will produce very little \nrain, with ensembles still maintaining a less than 20 percent chance \nof exceeding one tenth of an inch. However, these showers may bring \na few hundredths of an inch across portions of the region. \n\nSaturday afternoon, still expecting very warm temperatures across \nthe region, with highs pushing into the 90s. Another shortwave may \ntrack through the upper level flow, bringing low end precipitation \nchances during the afternoon hours. However, by the evening into the \nnighttime hours, precipitation chances will increase. Guidance \ncontinues to trend to the more progressive trough solutions, which \nis prompting the earlier and increasing trends in precipitation. \nEnsembles are even coming into better consensus, now bringing a 30 \npercent chance of exceeding one tenth of an inch Saturday night \nacross portions of western and north central Nebraska. \n\nThe upper level trough continues to track east on Sunday, bringing a \nfairly active weather pattern through at least mid week. As the \ntrough approaches on Sunday, expecting stronger upper level winds to \napproach the region, placing an area of divergence aloft. This will \nfurther help a surface low to deepen, increasing precipitation \nchances Sunday evening into the overnight hours. Ensemble guidance \nSunday night brings a 40 percent chance of precipitation exceeding \none tenth of an inch, which continues to be a slight upward trend \nfrom previous runs. \n\nAs the upper level trough continues to track to the northeast, \nexpecting to see slight ridging across the south, with the trough \nriding the north end of the ridge. This is expected to place the \nstronger upper level winds across South Dakota, with several \nshortwaves tracking through the flow. With the stronger upper level \nsupport, will see continued precipitation chances, especially in the \nevening and overnight hours through at least mid week. With subtle \nridging remaining across the southern United States, expect to see \nmore seasonal to slightly above seasonal temperatures return. In \ngeneral, expecting the warmer temperatures to remain across the \nsouthern portions of the region, but even then, this may be around 5 \ndegrees above normal.",
